<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Duration</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><strong class="text-amber-400">Not like-for-like.</strong> Ruby has no Duration type, so this compares Soli's <code class="text-cyan-400">Duration</code> objects against integer-second arithmetic. Shown for completeness; do not quote it.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">between</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">4.883</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.038</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.963</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.036</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">5.07&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">of_days</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">3.835</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.289</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.272</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.264</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">14.53&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Numeric</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Ruby's clearest win. Tight arithmetic loops are dominated by opcode dispatch, where Ruby's JIT-compiled loops show.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">float_math</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.626</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.579</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.493</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.528</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.30&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">int_loop</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.836</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.256</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.242</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.259</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.45&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">modulo</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.655</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.317</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.302</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.328</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">5.48&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Control</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Function and closure calls. YJIT/ZJIT compile these to machine code; Soli interprets every call.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">closure_call</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.790</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.641</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.529</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.601</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.38&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">fn_call</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.931</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.541</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.403</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.433</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">4.79&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Why the split exists</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">Soli's built-in methods are compiled Rust over native data structures, so an operation that never re-enters the interpreter runs at Rust speed. An operation that calls back into Soli once per element pays ~44 cycles per bytecode dispatch, and Ruby's JIT compiles the equivalent loop to machine code &mdash; an entire ZJIT iteration fits inside one Soli dispatch.</p>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-10">That is the whole story of this page. It is also why the fix for a slow Soli program is usually to reach for a built-in rather than to hand-write the loop.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What this does not measure</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-10">Micro-benchmarks of individual operations. They say nothing about end-to-end web request throughput, where framework architecture, the database and I/O dominate. Treat this as a guide to <em>which operations are cheap</em>, not a verdict on either language.</p>
<h2 id="corrections" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Corrections</h2>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5 mb-10"><p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">This page's first version reported an overall <strong class="text-white">0.99&times;</strong>. That was wrong: the input data flattered Soli.</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-3"><li><code class="text-cyan-400">sort</code> ran on <strong class="text-white">already-sorted</strong> input &mdash; Rust detects the ordered run and goes O(n); Ruby does not.</li><li><code class="text-cyan-400">intersection</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">difference</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">union</code> ran on two <strong class="text-white">identical</strong> arrays.</li><li><code class="text-cyan-400">flatten</code> ran on a <strong class="text-white">flat</strong> array, never exercising its recursion.</li><li><code class="text-cyan-400">uniq</code> ran on <strong class="text-white">all-unique</strong> input.</li></ul><p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">All fixed &mdash; shuffled LCG data, 50%-overlapping ranges, genuinely nested input, duplicate-heavy input. <code class="text-cyan-400">sort</code>, which had looked like a 5&times; win, is a real but modest one.</p></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Reproducing</h2>
<pre class="bg-black/40 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 overflow-x-auto mb-4"><code class="language-bash">soli --vm bench/cross-language/bench_all.sl
ruby        bench/cross-language/bench_all.rb   # add --yjit / --zjit</code></pre>
<p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Both print <code class="text-cyan-400">category|operation|best_ms</code>.</p>
